<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marcus Terentius Varro Lucullus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">116–c. 56 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Marcus Lucullus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marcus Terentius Varro Lucullus was a Roman politician and general, younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-licinius-lucullus">Lucius Licinius Lucullus</a>, who was consul in 73 BCE. As proconsul of Macedonia in 72–71 BCE he campaigned against the Thracian Bessi and brought the Greek cities of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast, among them Apollonia, Tomis, Odessos, Mesembria and Callatis, under Roman control, carrying a colossal statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as Marcus Licinius Lucullus and was later adopted by an otherwise unknown Marcus Terentius Varro, so that inscriptions give his name as M. Terentius M. f. Varro Lucullus; literary texts usually call him simply Lucullus, which has caused confusion with his brother. In the early 90s BCE the two brothers unsuccessfully prosecuted Servilius the Augur, who had earlier prosecuted their father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-licinius-lucullus">Lucius Licinius Lucullus</a>, and sent him into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucania">Lucania</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> returned from the East in 83 BCE, Marcus joined him and served as a legatus in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> under his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-pius">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius</a>. At Fidentia, with 15 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a>, he defeated a force of 50 cohorts under a legate of Gnaeus Papirius Carbo. He probably entered the Pontifical College when Sulla enlarged it in 81 BCE. As curule aedile in 79 BCE with his brother he gave games that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> long remembered, with revolving stage backdrops and the first fight of an elephant against a steer. As praetor peregrinus in 76 BCE he presided over the trial of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-antonius-hybrida">Gaius Antonius Hybrida</a>, prosecuted by the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>.</p><p>As consul, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-cassius-longinus">Gaius Cassius Longinus</a>, he passed the lex Terentia et Cassia frumentaria, which gave subsidised grain to poor citizens. As governor of Macedonia he campaigned against the Bessi, reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and took Greek cities that had been bases of Mithridates VI, and he held a triumph in 71 BCE. Recalled to fight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacus">Spartacus</a>, he landed at Brundisium, and the news turned Spartacus back to face <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> in the war's final battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 70 BCE he testified for the prosecution of Verres, and in 66 or 65 BCE he was tried by Gaius Memmius for his acts under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> and acquitted. He opposed Catilina in 63 BCE, was the main witness for the defence of the poet Licinius Archias, and in 58 and 57 BCE worked for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>'s return from exile. When his brother lost his mental powers, Marcus became his guardian, and he buried him at his Tusculan estate in 56 BCE, dying himself not long afterwards.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marcus Terentius Varro Lucullus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 72 BCE <b>Marcus Lucullus</b> captured Apollonia during the Third Mithridatic War and took the Apollo statue to the Capitol in Rome.</blockquote><a href="/state/apollonia-pontica">Apollonia Pontica</a></li><li><blockquote>After the campaign of <b>Marcus Terentius Varro Lucullus</b> in 72-71 BCE it lay within the Roman sphere and later in the province of Moesia.</blockquote><a href="/state/callatis">Callatis</a></li><li><blockquote>In 72 to 71 BCE <b>Marcus Lucullus</b> brought the western Pontic cities under Roman control, and Burebista's Getae sacked the city soon after.</blockquote><a href="/state/histria">Histria</a></li><li><blockquote>Roman forces under <b>Marcus Terentius Varro Lucullus</b> subdued the West Pontic cities in 72-71 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/mesembria">Mesembria</a></li><li><blockquote>Like Tomis, Callatis and Mesembria, Odessos came under Roman control after the campaign of <b>Marcus Terentius Varro Lucullus</b> in 72-71 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/odessos">Odessos</a></li><li><blockquote>The Roman general <b>Marcus Terentius Varro Lucullus</b> brought the West Pontic cities under Roman control in 72-71 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/tomis">Tomis</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-77" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Marcus Terentius Varro Lucullus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-77">What was here in 77 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
